{
 "_doc": "Hand decisions read by the pipeline. Each break entry means: a reading may end after this verse at the 'scene' cost (params: torah_break_costs.scene / nakh_break_costs.scene) instead of its normal, higher cost. If the verse already costs less (petuḥa, setuma, aliyah end) the entry has no effect. Verse numbers are WLC numbers; for Torah they are after the Decalogue renumbering (no entry falls in Exod 20 or Deut 5, so the two agree). 'opens_next' (the first words of the following verse, cantillation removed) was added when this file was written for the handoff so a reviewer can find the spot; the per-item reasons were not written down at the time, only the reason for each review round, given in 'reasons'. 'note' records what was said about an item in the conversation, where anything was.",
 "reasons": {
  "torah_r1": "Plan 1, first optimizer run: every day that ended at a bare chapter end or plain verse was listed, and a nearby narrative or topic division was hand-picked as an acceptable break point.",
  "torah_r2": "Plan 1, second review after rerun: days that still ended at a chapter end or plain verse near these points.",
  "torah_r3": "Plan 1, after adding the no-book-crossing constraint: one day in Gen 50 ended at a plain verse; these two were added.",
  "parasha": "Plan 2, first run: 13 days (6-day) and 11 days (5-day) ended at a chapter end or plain verse, almost all in short parashiyyot that must be split 6 or 5 ways; break points were added inside them. Shared with plan 1 lists, which still apply.",
  "nakh_r1": "Three-track run 1: Nakh days ending at chapter ends or plain verses were listed; scene breaks were added in prose passages. In the same step plain-verse breaks in poetic books were made dearer (verse_poetic) so poetry days fall back to chapter ends.",
  "nakh_r2": "Three-track run 2: two Daniʾel days in the 6-day plan still ended at a plain verse (Dan 3:26–4:10 and Dan 11:1–16); these were added."
 },
